# Line endings are not the platform's choice to make.
#
# The rule is one line: everything git detects as text is stored and checked
# out with LF, on every machine. `core.autocrlf` is true by default on Windows
# and this file overrides it — which is the point, because that setting is
# per-machine and the requirement is per-repository.
#
# This replaces a list that had grown one entry per outage. The outages are
# kept below as comments, because each one is evidence for the rule rather
# than a special case beside it:
#
#   *.sh, Dockerfile, *.service
#       Executed by Linux wherever they were checked out. Under a CRLF checkout
#       `#!/usr/bin/env bash` becomes `bash\r` and the container fails with
#       "bad interpreter".
#   mise.toml, *.yml, *.yaml
#       Its tasks are shell scripts run inside the container. `set -e` with a
#       trailing CR is "Illegal option -", and the Linux release never starts.
#   *.example
#       Copied to .env and read by Docker, which keeps the CR as part of the
#       value — a password ending in an invisible character nobody typed.
#   *.sql
#       Migrations are hash-pinned and a hash is over bytes. A Windows checkout
#       rewrote these with CRLF, `git archive` carried that to the server, the
#       recorded pin was the CRLF one, and the next deployment from Linux
#       presented the LF hash of a file nobody had edited. The runner refused
#       to continue and the operator was down until somebody read the message
#       closely enough to disbelieve it. The runner now pins content rather
#       than bytes; this file means it never has to again.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, named explicitly. Git's detection is a heuristic over the first
# 8000 bytes, and a heuristic that guesses "text" once corrupts the file.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.mov   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.xz    binary
*.deb   binary
*.dmg   binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.msi   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary

# Licence texts are verbatim by definition: not rewritten in either direction,
# on any platform. The committed bytes are LF and stay the bytes that ship.
LICENSE     -text
LICENSE-*   -text
